/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  kinetics02_h2o2_falloff -- the fall-off experiment: the first-order decay of
  a trace of H2O2 in N2 at 1000 K and 30 bar (choupoBatch).  The second witness
  of the EduTool `how-do-we-know-k`; its sibling is kinetics01_oh_decay.

  WHAT IT IS.  An isothermal closed vessel holds N2 and 1e-10 (mole fraction) of
  H2O2.  The reaction H2O2 + M = 2 OH + M (R15 of the O Conaire et al. 2004
  mechanism, Int. J. Chem. Kinet. 36, 603, Table I) is a Troe fall-off: its
  first-order constant k lies between the low-pressure limit k0 [M] and the
  high-pressure limit k_inf.  At 30 bar and 1000 K the reduced pressure
  P_r = k0 [M] / k_inf is about 0.67, near the middle of the curve, and
  ln n_H2O2 against t is a straight line of slope -k.  The H2O2 seed is small
  so that the OH it makes does not attack it (OH + H2O2 = H2O + HO2): at a
  seed of 1e-5 the decay reads about 1.8 times too fast (measured natively at
  1000 K, 1 and 30 bar, against the record's own k).  The window (5 ms) holds
  about eight lifetimes.

  WHAT THE PAGE DOES WITH IT.  It reruns the cell at other pressures and
  temperatures (declared numbers of 0/ and of this file), fits k at each, and
  draws the Lindemann and Troe laws of the record beside the measured points.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

application   choupoBatch;
description   "Fall-off cell: a trace of H2O2 decaying in N2 at 1000 K, 30 bar, isothermal (O Conaire 2004 H2/O2 kinetics, R15 Troe fall-off); the signal the EduTool how-do-we-know-k fits for k";

verbosity     3;

// ---- Time integration ----
startTime       0;          // s
endTime         5.0e-3;     // s  (about eight H2O2 lifetimes at the defaults)
deltaT          2.5e-5;     // s  (the stiff integrator sub-steps adaptively within each deltaT)
writeInterval   2.5e-5;     // s  (200 instants across the window)
